# Authnull on-premise — configuration
#
# Copy to .env and run ./install.sh, which fills in the generated section for you.
#
# THREE KINDS OF VALUE LIVE IN THIS FILE, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.
# Read the section headers before editing anything.
#
# This file is committed to a PUBLIC repository. Every secret value here is empty
# and must stay empty. A working value committed here is a working value for every
# customer who ever downloads the package.

# ==========================================================================
# 1. GENERATED — do not fill in by hand, do not ever commit a value
# ==========================================================================
#
# install.sh writes these with `openssl rand`, once, at install time.
# 
# If this package ever shipped fixed values here, every deployment in the world
# would share one JWT signing key -- so a session token minted on one customer's
# system would validate on another's. That is not a theoretical concern: the file
# is public, so the key would be public too.
# 
# Rotating these after install invalidates existing sessions and makes
# already-encrypted column values unreadable. Change them only deliberately.
#
# TWO OF THESE ARE NOT FREE-FORM STRINGS. If you ever set them by hand:
#
#   ENCRYPTION_KEY        EXACTLY 32 characters. It is passed straight to aes.NewCipher,
#                         so any other length fails at runtime -- not at startup -- the
#                         first time a credential is encrypted.
#   TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY   EXACTLY 64 hex characters (32 bytes decoded). Anything else and
#                         the service generates a random key at each boot, so TOTP secrets
#                         stop decrypting after a restart. That presents as data
#                         corruption, not as a configuration error.
#
# install.sh generates both correctly and refuses to continue if they are wrong.
#
DB_PASSWORD=
ENCRYPTION_KEY=
INTERNAL_API_KEY=
JWT_SECRET=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
REDIS_PASSWORD=
TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ==========================================================================
# 2. REQUIRED — you must set these; the install cannot guess them
# ==========================================================================
#
# URLs must be the addresses a BROWSER uses, not container names. SYSTEM_URL is
# the API base; CLIENT_URL is the console root. On a split deployment they differ,
# and enrollment links are built from CLIENT_URL -- point it at the API host and
# every invite link 404s in the browser.
# 
# DB_* describe the bundled Postgres. Leave the defaults unless you are pointing
# at an external database.
#
AUTHNULL_LOGOUT_URL=
CLIENT_URL=
DB_HOST=
DB_NAME=
DB_PORT=
DB_SCHEMA=
DB_USER=
DOMAIN_URL=
ORG_NAME=
ROOT_URL=
SSC_BASE_URL=
SSC_SIGNIN_URL=
SSC_URL=
SYSTEM_URL=
TENANT_ID=

# Sensible defaults for the bundled Postgres:
#   DB_HOST=postgres
#   DB_PORT=5432
#   DB_USER=authnull
#   DB_NAME=authnull
#   DB_SCHEMA=did

# ==========================================================================
# 3. OPTIONAL — features stay off until configured
# ==========================================================================
#
# Nothing here is needed to start the system or to enforce AD MFA.
# 
#   SMTP_*     outbound email: enrollment invites, password reset. Without it the
#              console works but cannot send mail.
#   OKTA_*     Okta as the MFA provider, and/or Okta SSO for console login.
#   DUO_*      Duo as the MFA provider.
#   TWILIO_*   SMS one-time codes.
#   MFA_*      push tuning: challenge TTL, risk scoring, geolocation.
#   WEBAUTHN_* security keys / platform authenticators.
# 
# MFA PROVIDER ON-PREM: the AuthNull Authenticator app is not available in a
# self-hosted deployment -- its push transport is FCM against Authnull's own
# Firebase project. Use Okta, Duo, TOTP or WebAuthn. All are supported by the same
# provider layer and need no Authnull-hosted component.
#
API_BASE_URL=
APP_ANDROID_URL=
APP_IOS_URL=
AUTHNULL_UPLINK_URL=
AUTHNZ_TIMEOUT_SEC=
DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=
DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=
DUO_HOST=
DUO_IKEY=
DUO_SKEY=
ENV=
ETHEREUM_URL=
FIX_TENANT_URL=
GIN_MODE=
INSTALL_SCRIPT_URL=
INTERNAL_API_BASE_URL=
IPINFO_TOKEN=
LOGGING_URL=
MFA_GEOIP_API_URL=
MFA_GEOIP_MODE=
MFA_GEOIP_TIMEOUT_MS=
MFA_PROVIDER=
# ── authn-service (the decision service) ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# How long a push may sit unanswered, and how often it is polled. These bound
# the whole chain: an enforcement point's own timeout must EXCEED the TTL, or
# the request dies before the user reaches their phone and it looks like a
# broken integration rather than a timeout. The SSH proxy's
# SSHPROXY_AUTHN_TIMEOUT_SEC defaults to 60 and must be raised alongside this.
#
# Blank here and the compose file's own defaults apply -- 60 seconds and 2000ms.
# Every value in this file is empty on purpose: it is public, and a value shipped
# here is a value somebody deploys without deciding to.
MFA_PUSH_CHALLENGE_TTL_SEC=
MFA_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=

# Optional. Empty means audit rows are written to the log volume and not shipped
# to a broker; authn-service logs each failed send and carries on.
KAFKA_BROKERS=
KAFKA_TOPIC=

MFA_PUSH_ACTIVITY_RETENTION_DAYS=
MFA_PUSH_ALERT_COOLDOWN_MIN=
MFA_PUSH_ALERT_TO=
MFA_PUSH_ALLOW_UNSIGNED=
MFA_PUSH_CHALLENGE_TTL_SEC=
MFA_PUSH_RISK_ENABLED=
MFA_PUSH_RISK_LOOKBACK_DAYS=
MFA_PUSH_SWEEP_ENABLED=
OKTA_API_TOKEN=
OKTA_DOMAIN=
OKTA_IDP_METADATA_URL=
ORG_DISCOVERY_ENABLED=
REDIS_PORT=
SMTP_BCC=
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_PORT=
SSO_VERIFICATION_URL=
SYSTEM_IP=
TENANT_BASE_DOMAIN=
TENANT_SERVICE_URL=
TRUSTED_PROXIES=
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=
VAULT_SERVICE_URL=
WALLET_ONBOARDING_REQUIRED=
WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=
WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=
